&l;span&g;Still, Cohen doesn&a;rsquo;t believe the game is over for Bitcoin and other major cryptocurrencies.&l;/span&g;&l;span&g;&a;ldquo;We&s;ve seen Bitcoin do this before, where it plunges sharply over a prolonged period only to violently bounce back to new highs in a short time.&a;nbsp;While it isn&s;t looking too hot at the moment, it&s;s still way to early too call the end of Bitcoin, or cryptos in general.&q;&l;/span&g;&l;span&g;&a;nbsp;&l;/span&g; 
Aaron Lasher, BRD&a;rsquo;s CMO and Co-founder, agrees. &q;The game isn&s;t over,&a;rdquo; says Lasher. &a;ldquo;Digital scarcity is a major innovation in money and value, and we&s;re in the initial stages of a multi-decade trend towards tokenization of assets.&a;rdquo; 
Lasher sees cryptocurrency innovations changing peoples&a;rsquo; lives. &a;ldquo;If sending money globally as easily as an email doesn&s;t impress you, how about the ability to store your life savings in your head, then walking your family across a war-torn border to safety?&a;rdquo; 
That&a;rsquo;s why people shouldn&a;rsquo;t be fooled by the recent social media gloom and doom about Bitcoin and other cryptocurrencies. &a;ldquo;Witness the continental shift towards cryptocurrency with a magnifying glass, and you might be fooled, but bitcoin has &q;died&q; over a hundred times in the past, at least according to traditional journalists.&a;rdquo; 
Lasher believes that Bitcoin and other cryptocurrencies are still the tickets to a rich and truly free life. &a;ldquo;Getting rich with cryptocurrency is a proxy for true freedom, a personal financial situation that is largely immune to the politics, flaws, and vicissitudes of an interconnected, global system – an oasis of security and a platform for individual pursuits.&q;&l;span&g;&a;nbsp;&l;/span&g; 
&l;span&g;These are certainly very comforting words to those investors who entered the cryptocurrency market near its old-time highs, but not to those who think that the cryptocurrency markets are in a bubble that is already bursting.&l;/span&g; 
